/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let Pin be the optical power launched into a fiber of length L km, and Pout the power that leaves the far end.
   - α = (10/L) log₁₀(Pin/Pout) α is the attenuation coefficient in dB/km; L is the fiber length in km; Pin and Pout are the input and output powers in the same unit; log₁₀ is the base-10 logarithm.
   - The loss over the whole length is A = αL in dB.
   - Two anchor points make the decibel easy.
   - Absolute power is quoted in dBm, decibels relative to 1 mW: P(dBm) = 10 log₁₀(P/1 mW).
   - Four families cover almost every loss in a fiber link.
   - Atotal = Aabs + Ascat + Abend + Ajoint Atotal is the total loss in dB; each term on the right is that family’s loss in dB.
   - The fiber terms are usually lumped into one coefficient α in dB/km, and the joints are counted as so many dB each.
   - Silica fiber is not equally clear at all wavelengths.
   - Long-haul links work at 1550 nm because each kilometre costs the fewest dB, so the spacing between amplifiers is longest; erbium-doped fiber amplifiers also work in this band.
*/
