/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Intrinsic absorption comes from the SiO₂ network itself, so even perfectly pure glass has it.
   - The ultraviolet tail is electronic.
   - The infrared tail is vibrational.
   - For a germania-doped silica core, two empirical formulas give the tails in dB/km, with λ in µm and x the mole fraction of GeO₂:
   - αUV = [154.2x/(46.6x + 60)] × 10−2 e4.63/λ αUV is the ultraviolet absorption in dB/km; x is the GeO₂ mole fraction (0.06 means 6 %); λ is the wavelength in µm.
   - αIR = 7.81 × 1011 e−48.48/λ αIR is the infrared absorption in dB/km; λ is in µm.
   - Extrinsic absorption comes from impurities.
   - The hydroxyl ion OH⁻ remains.
   - Two other sources are worth one line each.
   - The water peak is not a wall.
*/
