/* book Worked example 2: Q: An old cable measures 1.10 dB/km at 1383 nm. A low-water-peak fiber measures 0.26 dB/km at the same wavelength. For a 20 km coarse-WDM channel at 1383 nm, find the extra loss caused by the water peak and the fraction of power it removes.
   1. Excess coefficient: 1.10 − 0.26 = 0.84 dB/km.
   2. Excess loss over the link: 0.84 dB/km × 20 km = 16.8 dB.
   3. Power fraction kept: 10−16.8/10 = 10−1.68 = 0.0209.
   4. Fraction removed: 1 − 0.0209 = 0.979, that is 97.9 %.
   Answer: The water peak costs 16.8 dB on this channel and removes 97.9 % of the power that the low-water-peak fiber would deliver. */
